{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793741545,"nonce":"80sOwaBNj2bRF1sbwpGlY8KTJIE=","title":"Interpreter: reuse frame storage and call argument buffers","message":"Follow-up to 02245c49b6668b5842c75a7826d73fb8cf24e31aca8693d299ffa3311ba2ba99. NUC profiling after lazy symbol caching identifies per-frame value setup and teardown as a major remaining cost: extend_with 7.76%, ZyntaxValue clone 7.76%, drop glue 7.28%, and argument Vec collection 4.75% in the combined symbol-call fixture. Allocator functions and GC root BTreeMap insert/remove add further costs. These are sampled diagnostic shares, not predicted speedups or whole-program profiles.\n\nrun_frame creates regs, union_blocks and four OSR bookkeeping vectors; each call site builds an argument Vec. FrameRoots also boxes a view and registers/removes a root entry for each GC-visible frame. Prioritize reusable per-depth frame storage and small argument buffers, measuring real cold/tiered Lua and Python work as well as isolated call throughput. Cached storage must clear stale owned values and cease rooting dead objects; recursive calls, callbacks, error unwinding, weak references/finalizers and OSR transfer must remain correct. Retained capacity must be bounded. Do not simply leave stale roots in a pooled frame.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-interpreter-speed/{results.json,candidate-profile.txt,base-final-profile.txt}. NUC i5-1250P, release LLVM-enabled build but pure HIR interpreter benchmark, affinity 0,2,4,6, no Cargo/rustc during timing. With roots enabled, the one-symbol call remains 151.76 ns versus 103.22 ns without roots. This isolates root-related overhead, not the separate native-call stack clear tracked by 9a71873.","files":null}]}